\paragraph{Bradley-Terry analysis.}
As the primary pairwise analysis, we compute a regularized estimate of the formal win probability across all 173 valid comparisons (88 NLI-gated pairs $\times$ 2 position trials, excluding 3 invalid trials), including position-inconsistent pairs that the consistency filter discards, using a Bradley-Terry model \citep{bradley1952rank} with pair-level bootstrap confidence intervals.
The estimated formal win probability is 63.0\% (bootstrap 95\% CI: $[55.7\%, 70.3\%]$, $p = 0.0004$, 10{,}000 resamples); both CI bounds exceed the 50\% null, and the interval width (14.6pp) reflects the moderate sample size.
For comparison, the consistency-filtered sign test yields 73.9\% (34/46 consistent pairs, $p = 0.006$).
That the full-data estimate (63.0\%) is \emph{lower} than the filtered estimate (73.9\%) rules out the concern that the consistency filter inflates the effect.

